              <!--  What Is TB-500?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">What Is TB-500?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">TB-500 is a synthetic peptide associated with residues 17–23 of human Thymosin β4. The sequence commonly identified with TB-500 is LKKTETQ, with N-terminal acetylation producing the form referred to as acetylated TB-500 in analytical literature.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Thymosin β4 itself is a naturally occurring 43-amino-acid peptide and is one of the principal intracellular proteins involved in regulating pools of monomeric G-actin. Through its interaction with actin, Thymosin β4 has been investigated in connection with cell movement, cytoskeletal organisation and tissue biology.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">The central actin-binding region of Thymosin β4 has received particular attention in laboratory research. Studies have identified the LKKTETQ region as an important functional sequence associated with actin binding, endothelial-cell migration and angiogenic activity.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-2">Research into TB-500 and related Thymosin β4 sequences has therefore focused on several areas, including:</p>
                <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1 mb-3">
                  <li>Actin binding and cytoskeletal regulation</li>
                  <li>Cellular migration</li>
                  <li>Endothelial-cell activity</li>
                  <li>Angiogenesis</li>
                  <li>Wound-healing models</li>
                  <li>Tissue remodelling</li>
                  <li>Extracellular matrix activity</li>
                  <li>Inflammatory signalling</li>
                  <li>Cellular survival and differentiation</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Much of the available evidence relates to Thymosin β4 or experimental fragments derived from it, rather than clinical studies of TB-500 itself.</p>
              </section>

              <!--  How Is TB-500 Investigated in Research?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">How Is TB-500 Investigated in Research?</h2>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Actin Binding</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">One of the principal areas of interest surrounding TB-500 is its relationship with the actin cytoskeleton. Thymosin β4 functions as a G-actin-sequestering peptide, helping regulate the availability of actin monomers within cells. The LKKTETQ region represents the central actin-binding domain identified within Thymosin β4, making the sequence particularly relevant to laboratory investigations involving cytoskeletal organisation and cell movement.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Cell Migration</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Cell migration is another major area of research involving Thymosin β4 and its active regions. Experimental studies have examined endothelial-cell movement, fibroblast activity and other forms of cellular migration. These processes are important to researchers studying tissue remodelling, wound-healing biology and vascular development.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Angiogenesis</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Research has also investigated the relationship between Thymosin β4's actin-binding region and angiogenesis. Laboratory experiments have examined endothelial-cell adhesion and migration, capillary formation and vessel sprouting. Research published in the FASEB Journal identified the actin-binding region as an important component of the angiogenic activity observed with Thymosin β4.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Wound-Healing Models</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Thymosin β4 has been investigated in experimental wound-healing systems involving cell migration, tissue remodelling and vascular responses. The published literature includes both cell-based and animal studies investigating these processes. These findings provide a basis for further laboratory research, but they should not be interpreted as evidence of established therapeutic effectiveness for TB-500 in humans.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Cytoskeletal Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Because of the relationship between Thymosin β4 and G-actin, TB-500-related research can also be relevant to studies of the cellular cytoskeleton. Actin polymerisation and depolymerisation are fundamental to processes including cell movement, morphology and structural organisation. Researchers have therefore investigated β-thymosins as regulators of actin availability and cellular behaviour.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Vascular Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Thymosin β4 has also been examined in relation to vascular development and repair. Research has investigated processes including vasculogenesis, angiogenesis, endothelial migration, vascular remodelling and pericyte recruitment. The underlying mechanisms remain an active area of scientific investigation.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
