              <!--  What Does the Research Say?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">What Does the Research Say?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">A substantial amount of the scientific literature surrounding TB-500 relates to Thymosin β4, the naturally occurring peptide from which the TB-500 sequence is derived. This distinction is important when evaluating published evidence.</p>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Thymosin β4 and Actin</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Thymosin β4 has been extensively studied as an actin-binding peptide. Research indicates that it binds monomeric G-actin and influences the balance between actin monomers and filament formation. This provides a biological basis for investigating its effects on cellular movement and cytoskeletal activity. The central LKKTETQ region has been identified as an important actin-binding sequence. Experimental work has demonstrated that this region can reproduce aspects of the angiogenic and cell-migration activity associated with Thymosin β4.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Cell Migration and Tissue Biology</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Cell migration is a recurring theme within the Thymosin β4 literature. Researchers have investigated how β-thymosins influence cell movement, including endothelial-cell migration and other cellular processes involved in tissue development and repair. However, the mechanisms responsible for these effects are complex and are not completely understood.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Angiogenesis Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Laboratory studies have reported associations between Thymosin β4 and angiogenic processes. Research has investigated endothelial-cell adhesion, migration, tube formation and vessel sprouting, with the actin-binding region playing an important role in these experimental effects. Further reviews have examined the wider role of Thymosin β4 in vascular development, neovascularisation and vascular responses to injury.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Wound-Healing Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Animal and laboratory studies have investigated Thymosin β4 in wound-healing models, examining processes such as cell migration, blood-vessel formation, inflammation and tissue remodelling. However, these findings primarily concern Thymosin β4 and related experimental systems. They should not be presented as proof that TB-500 produces equivalent effects in humans.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Research Specifically Examining TB-500</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">More recent analytical research has examined TB-500 itself, including its metabolism and biological activity in experimental systems. A 2024 study investigated TB-500 and its metabolites using advanced mass-spectrometry techniques. The researchers noted that previously reported wound-healing activity associated with TB-500 may involve metabolites rather than necessarily being attributable to the parent peptide itself. This is an important consideration when interpreting claims surrounding TB-500 and demonstrates why the compound should continue to be evaluated through controlled laboratory research.</p>
